<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-09-27.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="133905"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#133905" label="[13:39:05]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: I agree removing the actual hardware from the bootstrap will be the biggest problem we face but I don't think the firmware bootstrap problem needs to be any harder than the Kernel bootstrap problem that builder-hex0 and fiwix beautifully solved.</span></div><div class="line" id="134222"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#134222" label="[13:42:22]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for example: we have <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/640-KB/GLaBIOS">https://github.com/640-KB/GLaBIOS</a>  to pull from</span></div><div class="line" id="134316"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#134316" label="[13:43:16]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and that is a FULL PC compatible BIOS that fits in 8KB</span></div><div class="line" id="134743"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#134743" label="[13:47:43]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and there are projects like this: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://hackaday.com/2022/03/30/building-your-own-80386dx-isa-single-board-microcomputer/">https://hackaday.com/2022/03/30/building-your-own-80386dx-isa-single-board-microcomputer/</a>  and the FPGA 486; which suggests that the seeds of bootstrapped hardware are not that far off;</span></div><div class="line" id="135029"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#135029" label="[13:50:29]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">The grab some Rubylith tape and create some photolithographic masks bootstrapping step requires much more steps prior completed first; and incrementally solving the BIOS problem for even a single board would be a real gain.</span></div><div class="line" id="135147"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#135147" label="[13:51:47]">&lt;nikolar&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and you don't even have to lay out the transistors by hand, just ask the toolchain to build you that fpga 486 :P</span></div><div class="line" id="135543"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#135543" label="[13:55:43]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nikolar: fair enough; but that is the thing about bootstrapping. We don't need perfect now/first. Just an incremental improvement that allows others to move closer to the desired perfect goal.</span></div><div class="line" id="135612"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#135612" label="[13:56:12]">&lt;nikolar&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and then you need someone who'll take all of the steps to prove that it can actually be done from scratch</span></div><div class="line" id="135704"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#135704" label="[13:57:04]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nikolar: ideally multiple different people who don't like each other and have every reason to point out that the other is mistaken.</span></div><div class="line" id="135713"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#135713" label="[13:57:13]">&lt;nikolar&gt;</a></span><span class="message">kek</span></div><div class="line" id="135944"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#135944" label="[13:59:44]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and we need only one example of one board with one bios that one could bootstrap to break that &quot;it can't be done&quot; story and open the field up.</span></div><div class="line" id="140433"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#140433" label="[14:04:33]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: and you need someone neutral to review</span></div><div class="line" id="140508"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#140508" label="[14:05:08]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: or someone who wants to tear it apart.</span></div><div class="line" id="140909"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#140909" label="[14:09:09]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">like just some hateful monster who spits vile crap and tries every possible way to tear down what you made; then listen the actual technical content of it and if there are technical issues pointed out =&gt; address them. If no technical issues are raised =&gt; we passed with flying colors.</span></div><div class="line" id="141124"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#141124" label="[14:11:24]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">like if they say: just doing something like x crashes the compiler. Thank them for the bug report and address the bug which would cause that crash and then look for related bugs to address as well.</span></div><div class="line" id="141216"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#141216" label="[14:12:16]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">If there is no technical weakness nor problem pointed out; they are just throwing shades of personal preference and that can just be disregarded without comment.</span></div><div class="line" id="141322"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#141322" label="[14:13:22]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Oh and before I forget; thank you aggi for your enhancements to TCC's usefulness in bootstrapping.</span></div><div class="line" id="141345"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#141345" label="[14:13:45]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: i haven't published yet</span></div><div class="line" id="141436"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#141436" label="[14:14:36]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">my goal is a 100% pure tcc-toolchain supported system</span></div><div class="line" id="141515"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#141515" label="[14:15:15]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I know but doing the work is hard and sometimes thankless and you deserve encouragement to continue the march.</span></div><div class="line" id="141546"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#141546" label="[14:15:46]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">just remember, when other people see your work, it is much easier for them to help you hit that goal.</span></div><div class="line" id="141636"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#141636" label="[14:16:36]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i don't want to cause too much noise and disturbance, and ideally can publish a _stable_ baseline</span></div><div class="line" id="141933"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#141933" label="[14:19:33]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and i got a few todos on my list which don't intersect with bootstrappable too</span></div><div class="line" id="141946"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#141946" label="[14:19:46]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: of course; who does decides and I look forward to it. (messy and chaos are just sometimes the growing pains of progress)</span></div><div class="line" id="142021"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#142021" label="[14:20:21]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for example, bearssl integration into the no-c++ system profile, because that's probably much easier to re-integrate with, once python/portage and perl/autotools are gone</span></div><div class="line" id="142116"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#142116" label="[14:21:16]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and finally, an i486-tcc-linux-musl.iso that is known good to work on almost any x86 system always, with all required development utilities contained</span></div><div class="line" id="142213"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#142213" label="[14:22:13]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the fact bootstrappable guarantees tcc-toolchain can be bootstrapped in compliance with acceptence criteria is a huge incentive to continue</span></div><div class="line" id="142239"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#142239" label="[14:22:39]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&quot;solving the BIOS problem&quot;?</span></div><div class="line" id="153759"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#153759" label="[15:37:59]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">andydude: good question</span></div><div class="line" id="153817"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#153817" label="[15:38:17]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fyi, tcc-toolchain hasn't got 16bit real-mode assembly support</span></div><div class="line" id="153855"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#153855" label="[15:38:55]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">binutils got, but those pull in a giant dependency graph of GNUism</span></div><div class="line" id="153903"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#153903" label="[15:39:03]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which isn't a licensing issue, it's a technical issue</span></div><div class="line" id="153933"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#153933" label="[15:39:33]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hence, please don't confuse my aversion towards GNU with GPL</span></div><div class="line" id="154004"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#154004" label="[15:40:04]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">GPL is ok, it doesn't interfere with interests</span></div><div class="line" id="154125"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#154125" label="[15:41:25]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but i am hesitant, to accept a gigantic dependency graph to process 16bit real-mode assembly for BIOS code</span></div><div class="line" id="154204"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#154204" label="[15:42:04]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's the circular-dependency issue, that i tried to introduce as a technical term (not political), that contradicts bootstrapping criteria</span></div><div class="line" id="154239"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#154239" label="[15:42:39]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as another side note, even when you approached 16bit systems that were known for free/open hardware SoC designs available</span></div><div class="line" id="154239"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#154239" label="[15:42:39]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">andydude: think how do you build a BIOS without having a BIOS</span></div><div class="line" id="154317"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#154317" label="[15:43:17]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in 16bit realm SDCC compile is commone, but that's written in, c++... circular dependency breaking clean bootstrapping</span></div><div class="line" id="154352"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#154352" label="[15:43:52]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">So even seabios is useless, because tcc can't compile it?</span></div><div class="line" id="154352"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#154352" label="[15:43:52]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sdcc compiler</span></div><div class="line" id="154408"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#154408" label="[15:44:08]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">andydude: simplified speaking, yes; but there's ore technical issues</span></div><div class="line" id="154413"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#154413" label="[15:44:13]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">more</span></div><div class="line" id="154431"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#154431" label="[15:44:31]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">andydude: it is more like, it is helpful for later portability but not the first trusted BIOS sort of situation.</span></div><div class="line" id="154621"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#154621" label="[15:46:21]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">what if seabios were just in hex0 format</span></div><div class="line" id="155156"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#155156" label="[15:51:56]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for their IBM PC, IBM considered the motorola mc68000 instead of Intel x86</span></div><div class="line" id="155212"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#155212" label="[15:52:12]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but this decision was made, in 1980s already</span></div><div class="line" id="155232"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#155232" label="[15:52:32]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so, what if, this question is obsolete</span></div><div class="line" id="155251"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#155251" label="[15:52:51]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">x86 it is what we got</span></div><div class="line" id="155301"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#155301" label="[15:53:01]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and their business habits</span></div><div class="line" id="155346"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#155346" label="[15:53:46]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">at Telekom universtity, they preferred to lecture 68k (20years ago, but this universtity closed in 2022)</span></div><div class="line" id="155418"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#155418" label="[15:54:18]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in the grand scheme of things, that's the whole Telekom/AT&amp;T vs. BSD dispute</span></div><div class="line" id="155449"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#155449" label="[15:54:49]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">wikipedia documents a few details, AT&amp;T had insisted the boot-code parts remain proprietary to them</span></div><div class="line" id="155539"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#155539" label="[15:55:39]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">GNU/binutils got a 16bit real-mode assembler, but it doesn't solve technical issues of bootstrapping from 16bit systems that are obsolete</span></div><div class="line" id="155727"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#155727" label="[15:57:27]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the 16bit systems (16bit address bus, 4/8bit ALU), never were sufficiently maintained</span></div><div class="line" id="155818"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#155818" label="[15:58:18]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i grew up behind iron curtain, when Intel insisted upon the COCOM and SCPA anti-competitive regulations</span></div><div class="line" id="155827"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#155827" label="[15:58:27]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's history</span></div><div class="line" id="155914"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#155914" label="[15:59:14]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">born in 1981, i didn't have a chance to study U880 clones in year 2000 anylonger, because at that time the former soviet/GDR semiconductor business was already defunct</span></div><div class="line" id="155947"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#155947" label="[15:59:47]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">instead, they introduced the Intel/Altera propriatary malice into Telekom University</span></div><div class="line" id="160027"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#160027" label="[16:00:27]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I recently found this article <a rel="nofollow" href="https://www.righto.com/2022/11/how-8086-processors-microcode-engine.html">https://www.righto.com/2022/11/how-8086-processors-microcode-engine.html</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="161713"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#161713" label="[16:17:13]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's fascinating to see how MUL is implemented with tons of ROLs</span></div><div class="line" id="161829"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#161829" label="[16:18:29]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I grew up on Mac, so I was exposed to 68k before x86</span></div><div class="line" id="161910"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#161910" label="[16:19:10]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">everything I learned about booting x86 I learned from tianocore/edk2</span></div><div class="line" id="162046"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#162046" label="[16:20:46]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the only part of it I remember now is <a rel="nofollow" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A20_line">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A20_line</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="162204"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#162204" label="[16:22:04]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">at Telekom University they lectured &quot;digital circuit design&quot;, 68k assembly</span></div><div class="line" id="162221"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#162221" label="[16:22:21]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but this unversity closed 2022, indefinitely</span></div><div class="line" id="162245"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#162245" label="[16:22:45]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i don't miss it</span></div><div class="line" id="162256"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#162256" label="[16:22:56]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and their contracting and business habits</span></div><div class="line" id="162332"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#162332" label="[16:23:32]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">drowning in proprieatary vendor malice</span></div><div class="line" id="162407"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#162407" label="[16:24:07]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh yes, the infamous Gate A20</span></div><div class="line" id="162457"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#162457" label="[16:24:57]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">had &quot;fun&quot; time handling that in builder-hex0</span></div><div class="line" id="162619"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#162619" label="[16:26:19]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">at least it's simplified by only supporting systems with BIOS-controlled Gate A20, or none at all (recent systems since Haswell/Bulldozer)</span></div><div class="line" id="162645"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#162645" label="[16:26:45]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Science is supposed to be about keeping an open mind, and data-driven hypothesis, not vendor hatred.</span></div><div class="line" id="163138"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#163138" label="[16:31:38]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">just received a few more extortion letters from financial departent</span></div><div class="line" id="163151"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#163151" label="[16:31:51]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that intended to sponsor Intel FABs with 12billion cash</span></div><div class="line" id="163231"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#163231" label="[16:32:31]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Intel fab construction in Magdeburg, terminated indefinitely</span></div><div class="line" id="163236"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#163236" label="[16:32:36]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, if it's aimed at building fabs in Europe, I'm down for it, even if it's Intel</span></div><div class="line" id="163253"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#163253" label="[16:32:53]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Only having high-end fabs in Taiwan is a huge global vulnerability</span></div><div class="line" id="163306"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#163306" label="[16:33:06]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i was fighting this tooth and nail</span></div><div class="line" id="163357"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#163357" label="[16:33:57]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">although my influence is marginal</span></div><div class="line" id="163414"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#163414" label="[16:34:14]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I wonder what's the best node today that can be fabbed outside Taiwan in existing facilities</span></div><div class="line" id="163451"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#163451" label="[16:34:51]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i wonder, why they didn't talk to Lattice Semi</span></div><div class="line" id="163630"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#163630" label="[16:36:30]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or, how could projects such as #bootstrappable do all their work, without 12billion subsidy cash</span></div><div class="line" id="163850"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#163850" label="[16:38:50]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">whatabout, why would competetent telco management be necessary, another one, for the lulz</span></div><div class="line" id="163918"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#163918" label="[16:39:18]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but i don't want to drag anyone into this</span></div><div class="line" id="163932"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#163932" label="[16:39:32]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's just, i received another extortion letter today</span></div><div class="line" id="163954"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#163954" label="[16:39:54]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that routes cash into &quot;climate transformation fund&quot; to sponsor Intel and Elon Musk with</span></div><div class="line" id="164004"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#164004" label="[16:40:04]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and a few other wish thinks</span></div><div class="line" id="164040"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#164040" label="[16:40:40]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and i am drowning in another pile of **** of government corruption</span></div><div class="line" id="164051"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#164051" label="[16:40:51]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I just watched ThePrimagen's video about the Automattic extortion ring</span></div><div class="line" id="164206"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#164206" label="[16:42:06]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">imagine if there was competent management, to coordinate and route fundings to those who deserve it</span></div><div class="line" id="164220"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#164220" label="[16:42:20]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Automattic asked WP-Engine for $30 million, WP-Engine said no. Automattic proceeded to launch a smear campaign against WP-Engine.</span></div><div class="line" id="164352"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#164352" label="[16:43:52]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but, if there was cash dedicated to projects, a mob of me-too follows along with it</span></div><div class="line" id="164419"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#164419" label="[16:44:19]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if i could, all that i asked for, was a few credible accounting records made, by projects such as #bootstrappable</span></div><div class="line" id="164457"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#164457" label="[16:44:57]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's how many man hours of work you invested</span></div><div class="line" id="164512"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#164512" label="[16:45:12]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's the money you get</span></div><div class="line" id="164533"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#164533" label="[16:45:33]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but that's all past and long gone</span></div><div class="line" id="164534"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#164534" label="[16:45:34]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Sounds like a meritocracy</span></div><div class="line" id="164734"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#164734" label="[16:47:34]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: last time I checked the lowest common denominator is still 14nm</span></div><div class="line" id="164807"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#164807" label="[16:48:07]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">j-core.org had considered another one</span></div><div class="line" id="164827"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#164827" label="[16:48:27]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">they said, 100nm or something was affordable and available</span></div><div class="line" id="164918"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#164918" label="[16:49:18]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">andydude: are there any still operational 14nm fabs outside Taiwan that the world can fall back to in the event of a Chinese invasion?</span></div><div class="line" id="164937"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#164937" label="[16:49:37]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">because that's my main concern here</span></div><div class="line" id="164955"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#164955" label="[16:49:55]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: what's wrong with 100nm?</span></div><div class="line" id="165021"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#165021" label="[16:50:21]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">2024-09-27 14:49:55 &lt;aggi&gt; Googulator: what's wrong with 100nm?</span></div><div class="line" id="165053"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#165053" label="[16:50:53]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://www.asml.com/en/products/euv-lithography-systems">https://www.asml.com/en/products/euv-lithography-systems</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="165054"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#165054" label="[16:50:54]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to my knowledge j-core.org were the last free/opensource project to approach it</span></div><div class="line" id="165108"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#165108" label="[16:51:08]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but their turtle board never went into production</span></div><div class="line" id="165134"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#165134" label="[16:51:34]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: ASML will send you a shipping container with a fab in it</span></div><div class="line" id="165153"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#165153" label="[16:51:53]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">pretty sure that counts as &quot;outside Taiwan&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="165225"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#165225" label="[16:52:25]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">was 100nm manufacturing available &quot;outside Taiwan&quot;?</span></div><div class="line" id="165302"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#165302" label="[16:53:02]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's good to know :) I was thinking, if panic buttons are pressed, humanity loses all relevant silicon fab capability for decades - but I guess that's not entirely the case</span></div><div class="line" id="165323"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#165323" label="[16:53:23]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">5nm might be lost to history, but we'll still have 14nm</span></div><div class="line" id="165458"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#165458" label="[16:54:58]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">14nm is good enough to restart from, at least - 100nm not so much</span></div><div class="line" id="165624"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#165624" label="[16:56:24]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: j-core.org didn't consider any of the modern manufacturing</span></div><div class="line" id="165639"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#165639" label="[16:56:39]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">they said, 100nm or something was cheap and available, for free/open hardware SoC</span></div><div class="line" id="165712"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#165712" label="[16:57:12]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and, hell yes, an i386 SMP clocked to 500MHz or something, that would be awesome</span></div><div class="line" id="165726"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#165726" label="[16:57:26]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm not talking about for open hardware development, but rather, a node the world can fall back to if Taiwan is invaded</span></div><div class="line" id="165837"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#165837" label="[16:58:37]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: that's the former soviet semiconductors, that detonated in 1986 when Chernobyl went boom</span></div><div class="line" id="165944"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#165944" label="[16:59:44]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&quot;tell me, you're a nuclear engineer such as myself, how does an RBMK reactor explode?&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="170048"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#170048" label="[17:00:48]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">huh ASML is headquartered in the Netherlands</span></div><div class="line" id="170119"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#170119" label="[17:01:19]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">andydude: the Zeiss optics manufacturer is headquarter in Thuringia, a few miles away from my home</span></div><div class="line" id="170149"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#170149" label="[17:01:49]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">however, i didn't study physics and laser optics</span></div><div class="line" id="170159"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#170159" label="[17:01:59]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i merely follow news</span></div><div class="line" id="170239"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#170239" label="[17:02:39]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the Z80 clone manufacturing, was located in Erfurt city, Thuringia</span></div><div class="line" id="170310"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#170310" label="[17:03:10]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's former GDR, a little distant to Chernobyl and Belarus and their facilities</span></div><div class="line" id="170314"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#170314" label="[17:03:14]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think the closest fab to me is GlobalFoundries in NY</span></div><div class="line" id="170613"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#170613" label="[17:06:13]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Where do I go for 100nm?</span></div><div class="line" id="170703"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#170703" label="[17:07:03]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">andydude: ask j-core.org</span></div><div class="line" id="170709"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#170709" label="[17:07:09]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well 230nm is good enough for a 64bit processor and 4+GB of RAM</span></div><div class="line" id="170722"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#170722" label="[17:07:22]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that was the last known free/open GPL-compliant project i knew</span></div><div class="line" id="170803"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#170803" label="[17:08:03]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and if one isn't requiring high yields or fast production; we could design a bootstrappable lithography lab for under $10K</span></div><div class="line" id="170925"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#170925" label="[17:09:25]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">The initial steps might be annoying if one expects to bootstrap all of the chips in that lithography lab first too</span></div><div class="line" id="171032"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#171032" label="[17:10:32]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and is purchased silicon wafer blanks and chemical acceptable might become a question if one wants perfect and complete knowledge of every detail of the process.</span></div><div class="line" id="171149"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#171149" label="[17:11:49]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but we can deal with that later as we have <a rel="nofollow" href="https://libresilicon.com/">https://libresilicon.com/</a>  giving a process good enough for 32bit CPUs (or slow 64bit CPUs)</span></div><div class="line" id="171212"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#171212" label="[17:12:12]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">depends on whether it's possible to hide a backdoor in blank wafers or chemicals</span></div><div class="line" id="174356"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#174356" label="[17:43:56]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: depends if we are worried about defending against nanoscale assemblers</span></div><div class="line" id="174713"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#174713" label="[17:47:13]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Pedagogy is more viral than Security</span></div><div class="line" id="174743"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#174743" label="[17:47:43]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">imho</span></div><div class="line" id="183710"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#183710" label="[18:37:10]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">andydude: well long term incremental projects can appear to be quite insane from the outside; but the results are thus far exactly as specified. #bootstrappable will just slowly grow until the answer to the question of how to go from nothing to a fully complete working computer with software is completely answered to the level desired by any individual. With the various forks and offshots to address various wants and needs; which make</span></div><div class="line" id="183710"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#183710" label="[18:37:10]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the community larger and richer in potential solutions to problems that may arise.</span></div><div class="line" id="185252"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#185252" label="[18:52:52]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: I understand 0% of the words on that website</span></div><div class="line" id="185400"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#185400" label="[18:54:00]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">andydude: j-core.org? they implemented a 100% free/open hardware SoC GPL compliant, but they're not compliant with bootstrapping criteria</span></div><div class="line" id="185401"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#185401" label="[18:54:01]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">what is SuperH</span></div><div class="line" id="185422"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#185422" label="[18:54:22]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">andydude: a non-x86 architecture, they had chosen because it's supported by GNU gcc/binutils</span></div><div class="line" id="185520"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#185520" label="[18:55:20]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to my understanding, they got 100% free/open non-patent incumbered schematics for hardware and tooling ready to deploy it</span></div><div class="line" id="185525"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#185525" label="[18:55:25]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Why not RiscV?</span></div><div class="line" id="185527"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#185527" label="[18:55:27]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">although they were whining about Xilinx</span></div><div class="line" id="185541"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#185541" label="[18:55:41]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">what is Xilinx?</span></div><div class="line" id="185554"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#185554" label="[18:55:54]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">andydude: because RISC-V isn't fully supported by GNU, and you would need most recent kernels/toolchains</span></div><div class="line" id="185618"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#185618" label="[18:56:18]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">andydude: Xilinx is an AMD subsidiary, similar to Altera that is married to Intel</span></div><div class="line" id="185625"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#185625" label="[18:56:25]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for hardware development</span></div><div class="line" id="185815"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#185815" label="[18:58:15]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">besides, a few hackers in the realm of j-core too bothered with tcc-toolchain, that's how they caught my attention</span></div><div class="line" id="185914"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#185914" label="[18:59:14]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i am not involved with disputes over GPLv3, although it seems a big deal among them</span></div><div class="line" id="185919"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#185919" label="[18:59:19]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">don't ask me why that is</span></div><div class="line" id="185959"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#185959" label="[18:59:59]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">a guy named Rob Langley was the author of busybox, but chose to fork it into toybox, for aboriginal linux distribtion of his</span></div><div class="line" id="190006"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#190006" label="[19:00:06]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which wasn't maintained for a decade since then</span></div><div class="line" id="190046"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#190046" label="[19:00:46]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in practice, there's no way around x86, although it sucks</span></div><div class="line" id="190108"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#190108" label="[19:01:08]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's why, j-core project has less relevance than i would appreciate</span></div><div class="line" id="190345"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#190345" label="[19:03:45]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">j-core.org wanted both, free/open hardware and software, chose SuperH instead of x86, and failed</span></div><div class="line" id="190431"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#190431" label="[19:04:31]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it sounds like they succeeded in a goal no one cares about</span></div><div class="line" id="190526"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#190526" label="[19:05:26]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">except for the fact, year 2023 german governent intended to dump 12billion of cash of subsidies into Intel Fabs</span></div><div class="line" id="190541"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#190541" label="[19:05:41]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">how much money had j-core.org needed to do their thing?</span></div><div class="line" id="190655"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#190655" label="[19:06:55]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">they had published some hacker talks, they could spawn some hardware manufacturing on &quot;deprecated&quot; equipment, for a few thousands of cash</span></div><div class="line" id="190739"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#190739" label="[19:07:39]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but their turtle board never was available, although it was some precious thing</span></div><div class="line" id="190845"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#190845" label="[19:08:45]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it didn't fit into bootstrappable criteria 100%, but at least they were 100% GPL compliant</span></div><div class="line" id="190924"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#190924" label="[19:09:24]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and they said, it was a few thousand cash required only, not billions of it</span></div><div class="line" id="190956"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#190956" label="[19:09:56]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's why, i got mad over the Intel subsisides in Magdeburg, who announced they'll not establish their Fabs, few days ago</span></div><div class="line" id="191000"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#191000" label="[19:10:00]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">VICTORY!~</span></div><div class="line" id="191017"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#191017" label="[19:10:17]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yet, why talk to me?</span></div><div class="line" id="191047"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#191047" label="[19:10:47]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ACTION has to refrain diverting into politics</span></div><div class="line" id="191122"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#191122" label="[19:11:22]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">because, i had supported some Intel deals made, if they had not ignored the free/open people including the ones here</span></div><div class="line" id="191132"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#191132" label="[19:11:32]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://buildd.debian.org/stats/graph-week.png">https://buildd.debian.org/stats/graph-week.png</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="191202"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#191202" label="[19:12:02]">&lt;andydude&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aide from a 3% difference, riscv looks fully supported to me</span></div><br /></div></body></html>